examples.question_text:
Classify each quadrilateral as convex or non-convex. If an interior angle is reflex, name its vertex.
(a)
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75,every node/.style={font=\normalsize,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0)--(3.5,0.5)--(4,2.8)--(0.8,3)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(b)
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75,every node/.style={font=\normalsize,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0)--(4.2,1.5)--(1.6,1.7)--(0.9,3.6)--cycle;
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$A$};
\node[right] at (4.2,1.5) {$B$};
\node[above right=4pt] at (1.6,1.7) {$C$};
\node[above] at (0.9,3.6) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Use the diagonals to classify each quadrilateral.
(a) Both diagonals are drawn.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75,every node/.style={font=\normalsize,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0)--(3.5,0.5)--(4,2.8)--(0.8,3)--cycle;
\draw[black,dashed] (0,0)--(4,2.8);
\draw[black,dashed] (3.5,0.5)--(0.8,3);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(b) Both diagonals are drawn.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75,every node/.style={font=\normalsize,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0)--(4.2,1.5)--(1.6,1.7)--(0.9,3.6)--cycle;
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$A$};
\node[right] at (4.2,1.5) {$B$};
\node[above right=4pt] at (1.6,1.7) {$C$};
\node[above] at (0.9,3.6) {$D$};
\draw[black,dashed] (0,0)--(1.6,1.7);
\draw[black,dashed] (4.2,1.5)--(0.9,3.6);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(c) Lee says the quadrilateral in (b) is convex because diagonal $AC$ lies inside it. Explain why this is wrong.
